# CKli convention: every text file is UTF-8 WITHOUT a BOM and is checked out with the line endings
# of the platform: CRLF on Windows, LF everywhere else.
#
# "text=auto" makes git store LF in the repository and write the newline of the platform in the
# working folder. That newline depends on the "core.autocrlf" and "core.eol" settings of the
# machine: "ckli issue" reports the settings that give the wrong one.
#
# Consequence: a multi-line C# string literal has the newline of the machine that compiles it.
# Code and tests must not depend on it.
#
# Git cannot express the "no BOM" half of the convention: that one stays a review rule.
* text=auto

# Fixed line endings, on every platform: the program that reads these files accepts only one.
# A shell fails on a CRLF script.
*.sh text eol=lf
# cmd.exe can miss the labels of an LF script.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Binary content: never convert, never diff.
*.png binary
*.zip binary
*.nupkg binary
*.ico binary
*.snk binary
